<!-- Website teaching layer for android/build-tooling/gradle-and-build-systems.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Sources checked 2026-10-25: Gradle build lifecycle, build cache, java-library (api vs implementation),
     version catalogs; Kotlin incremental compilation; Android "Migrate to built-in Kotlin". -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">You change one string in a feature screen and press Run. Forty seconds pass before anything compiles, and then the build compiles modules you never touched. Your teammate says "just run clean". That might make the next build pass. It won't tell you where the forty seconds went.</p>

Gradle runs a build in three phases: it finds the projects, configures them into a graph of tasks, and runs the tasks that graph needs. Each can be slow for different reasons. This chapter covers the graph, the plugins that fill it, and the boundaries that decide how far one edit spreads. The additions here help you find which part of a slow build to fix first.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### Three phases, three different costs

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Initialization</strong><small><code>settings.gradle.kts</code> decides which projects take part</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Configuration</strong><small>build scripts and plugins run; tasks are registered and wired</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Task graph</strong><small>only the tasks the requested goal needs</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Execution</strong><small>tasks run in dependency order; independent ones may overlap</small></div></div><figcaption>Gradle's documented build lifecycle. A slow build can be slow before the first task starts: time spent in the first two boxes is not compilation.</figcaption></figure>

